ch1.math-1-103:
Start with the rule that has the longest reach. Ben brings Carla in by rule (1), and Carla pushes Dan out by rule (3):

$$\text{Ben} \Rightarrow \text{Carla} \Rightarrow \neg\,\text{Dan}.$$

So a game with Ben in it never has Dan. Read from the other end the same fact says: if Dan plays then Carla stays out, which is rule (3)'s contrapositive, and with no Carla there can be no Ben either.

**With Ben playing:** Carla joins, Dan is out, rule (4) is already satisfied by Ben, so Ella may please herself. Two rosters.

**Without Ben:** rule (4) forces Ella in. Carla and Dan cannot both play, so we may take Carla, or Dan, or neither. Three rosters, and in the Dan one rule (2) is content because Ella is there.

The complete list:

• {Ben, Carla}

• {Ben, Carla, Ella}

• {Carla, Ella}

• {Dan, Ella}

• {Ella}

Two things are worth reading off that list. Ella alone is legal, since with Ben, Carla and Dan away three of the rules ask for nothing and Ella satisfies rule (4) by herself. And Carla is missing from two of the five rosters, so nobody can call her compulsory.
